  "body": "﻿## What happens\n\nImporting a 3.7 MB STEP file of a twisted rotor passes the kernel import's 300 s bound and cancels. The import log line:\n\n```\nWARN camcraft_geometry::kernel_import: STEP import passed its 300s bound; cancelling\n```\n\nFile: Rotor.STEP, 3,723,412 bytes, twisted rotor with curved faces. Retry gives the same result (deterministic).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nA user with a legitimate twisted-geometry part cannot import it at all through the STEP path - the only path for B-rep. The import does not resume or part-complete; the whole operation cancels.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nImport Rotor.STEP (available from the reporter) on Windows x64. The import runs the full 300 s, then the cooperative cancellation fires.\n\n## Possible directions\n\n- Raise or make the bound configurable is not the fix - the import is kernel-bound, single-threaded B-rep sewing. 300 s was already a generous budget.\n- Profile where the kernel time goes (sewing? facetting? the fit stage of #2718/#2754?) and see whether the curved-face boundary splitting dominates.\n- An interim escape: the user exports a mesh from the CAD side and imports STL/OBJ, which is fast - but loses B-rep fidelity for the fidelity checks.\n\nSuggested labels: P3, area:geometry, effort:high, source:agent.\r\n",
